<div class="report-container">

<!-- HEADER -->
<header class="report-header">
  <h1>Lichess Chess Game Analytics</h1>
  <p class="subtitle">Patterns of Victory, Openings, and Player Dynamics &mdash; A comprehensive analysis of 20,058 games exploring win rates, opening strategies, rating dynamics, and game outcome patterns.</p>
  <div class="meta">Dataset: Lichess.org (Kaggle &bull; datasnaek/chess) &nbsp;|&nbsp; Games analyzed: 20,058 &nbsp;|&nbsp; Report generated: April 2026</div>
  <ul class="toc">
    <li><a href="#overview">Overview</a></li>
    <li><a href="#color-advantage">Color Advantage</a></li>
    <li><a href="#rating-analysis">Rating Dynamics</a></li>
    <li><a href="#opening-strategy">Opening Analysis</a></li>
    <li><a href="#time-control">Time Controls</a></li>
    <li><a href="#key-findings">Key Findings</a></li>
  </ul>
</header>

<!-- TOP-LINE KPIs -->
<div class="kpi-row">
  <div class="kpi-card">
    <div class="kpi-value">20,058</div>
    <div class="kpi-label">Total Games</div>
  </div>
  <div class="kpi-card white-kpi">
    <div class="kpi-value">52.3%</div>
    <div class="kpi-label">White Win Rate (Decisive)</div>
  </div>
  <div class="kpi-card green-kpi">
    <div class="kpi-value">60.5</div>
    <div class="kpi-label">Avg. Game Length (Turns)</div>
  </div>
  <div class="kpi-card">
    <div class="kpi-value">31.5%</div>
    <div class="kpi-label">Checkmate Rate</div>
  </div>
  <div class="kpi-card draw-kpi">
    <div class="kpi-value">4.7%</div>
    <div class="kpi-label">Draw Rate</div>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<!-- SECTION 1: DATASET OVERVIEW -->
<!-- ============================================================ -->
<section class="section" id="overview">
  <h2>1. Dataset Overview &amp; Game Distribution</h2>
  <p class="section-desc">A high-level summary of the dataset composition, game outcomes, and termination types across all 20,058 games.</p>

  <div class="kpi-row">
    <div class="kpi-card">
      <div class="kpi-value">80.5%</div>
      <div class="kpi-label">Rated Games (16,155)</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card">
      <div class="kpi-value">19.5%</div>
      <div class="kpi-label">Casual Games (3,903)</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card">
      <div class="kpi-value">1,597</div>
      <div class="kpi-label">Avg. Player Rating</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card">
      <div class="kpi-value">784–2,723</div>
      <div class="kpi-label">Rating Range</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h3>Winner Distribution</h3>
  <p>Of all 20,058 games: <strong>10,001 (49.9%)</strong> were won by White, <strong>9,107 (45.4%)</strong> by Black, and <strong>950 (4.7%)</strong> ended in draws.</p>
  <div class="bar-meter">
    <div class="white-bar" style="width:49.86%">White 49.9%</div>
    <div class="black-bar" style="width:45.4%">Black 45.4%</div>
    <div class="draw-bar" style="width:4.74%">4.7%</div>
  </div>

  <h3>Victory Status Breakdown</h3>
  <p>How games end tells us about the player population and time controls. Resignation dominates, reflecting the intermediate-to-advanced rating tier of most players.</p>

  <div class="chart-row">
    <div class="chart-container">
      <canvas id="victoryStatusPie"></canvas>
    </div>
    <div class="chart-container">
      <canvas id="winnerPie"></canvas>
    </div>
  </div>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Victory Status</th><th class="num">Games</th><th class="num">% of Total</th><th class="num">Avg. Turns</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Resign</td><td class="num">11,147</td><td class="num">55.6%</td><td class="num">53.9</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Checkmate</td><td class="num">6,325</td><td class="num">31.5%</td><td class="num">65.4</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Out of Time</td><td class="num">1,680</td><td class="num">8.4%</td><td class="num">72.7</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Draw</td><td class="num">906</td><td class="num">4.5%</td><td class="num">83.8</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <div class="insight">
    <strong>Note:</strong> The draw rate in this Lichess dataset (4.7%) is substantially lower than in professional over-the-board chess (~50%+), reflecting the online amateur context where players are more likely to play on in drawish positions. Out-of-time finishes average the longest games (72.7 turns), suggesting these are often closely contested battles.
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<!-- SECTION 2: WHITE VS BLACK - FIRST MOVE ADVANTAGE -->
<!-- ============================================================ -->
<section class="section" id="color-advantage">
  <h2>2. White vs. Black: The First-Move Advantage</h2>
  <p class="section-desc">Quantifying the most debated question in chess — does moving first confer a meaningful advantage? We examine this across the entire dataset, then slice by victory type, rating bracket, and time control.</p>

  <h3>Overall Color Advantage</h3>
  <p>Excluding draws, White wins <strong>52.34%</strong> of decisive games (10,001 of 19,108) vs. Black's <strong>47.66%</strong> (9,107). This <strong>4.68 percentage point gap</strong> translates to White winning roughly <strong>1.10 games for every 1 Black win</strong> — a consistent, meaningful edge.</p>

  <div class="bar-meter" style="max-width:600px">
    <div class="white-bar" style="width:52.34%">White 52.3%</div>
    <div class="black-bar" style="width:47.66%">Black 47.7%</div>
  </div>

  <h3>Color Advantage by Victory Type</h3>
  <p>White's advantage is not uniform across ending types. It is strongest in checkmates and resignations, but <strong>disappears entirely in timeout finishes</strong>.</p>

  <div class="chart-container">
    <canvas id="colorByVictoryStatus"></canvas>
  </div>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Victory Type</th><th class="num">White Wins</th><th class="num">Black Wins</th><th class="num">White %</th><th class="num">Black %</th><th class="num">White Edge</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Checkmate</td><td class="num">3,344</td><td class="num">2,981</td><td class="num">52.9%</td><td class="num">47.1%</td><td class="num" style="color:var(--accent3)">+5.7 pp</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Resign</td><td class="num">5,844</td><td class="num">5,303</td><td class="num">52.4%</td><td class="num">47.6%</td><td class="num" style="color:var(--accent3)">+4.9 pp</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Out of Time</td><td class="num">813</td><td class="num">823</td><td class="num">49.7%</td><td class="num">50.3%</td><td class="num" style="color:var(--accent2)">−0.6 pp</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <div class="insight success">
    <strong>Key Finding:</strong> White's first-move advantage manifests in positional and tactical superiority (checkmate +5.7 pp, resignation +4.9 pp), but <strong>clock pressure is color-blind</strong> — timeout results are essentially 50/50, suggesting time management skill is independent of color.
  </div>

  <h3>Color Advantage by Rating Bracket</h3>
  <p>A critical question: does the first-move advantage change with player strength? The data reveals a clear trend — <strong>White's edge diminishes as average game rating increases</strong>.</p>

  <div class="chart-container">
    <canvas id="colorByRating"></canvas>
  </div>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Rating Bracket</th><th class="num">Games</th><th class="num">White Win %</th><th class="num">White Edge</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>&lt;1200</td><td class="num">1,169</td><td class="num">52.7%</td><td class="num" style="color:var(--accent3)">+5.3 pp</td></tr>
      <tr class="highlight-row"><td>1200–1399</td><td class="num">3,677</td><td class="num">53.8%</td><td class="num" style="color:var(--accent3)">+7.6 pp</td></tr>
      <tr><td>1400–1599</td><td class="num">6,066</td><td class="num">52.2%</td><td class="num" style="color:var(--accent3)">+4.3 pp</td></tr>
      <tr><td>1600–1799</td><td class="num">4,680</td><td class="num">52.2%</td><td class="num" style="color:var(--accent3)">+4.4 pp</td></tr>
      <tr><td>1800–1999</td><td class="num">2,967</td><td class="num">51.8%</td><td class="num" style="color:var(--accent3)">+3.6 pp</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2000+</td><td class="num">1,499</td><td class="num">50.6%</td><td class="num" style="color:var(--accent3)">+1.2 pp</td></tr>
    </tbody>
