<section class="section" id="key-findings">
  <h2>6. Key Findings and Strategic Takeaways</h2>
  <p class="section-desc">A synthesis of the most actionable insights from across all analyses.</p>

  <div class="findings-grid">
    <div class="finding-card">
      <h4>1. White's Edge is Real but Modest</h4>
      <p>White wins 52.3% of decisive games — a consistent 4.7 pp advantage. This edge is strongest at intermediate ratings (1200–1400) and nearly vanishes above 2000, suggesting stronger players better exploit Black's defensive resources.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="finding-card">
      <h4>2. Rating Trumps Everything</h4>
      <p>A 200-point rating advantage yields a 75% win probability. At 300+ points, the stronger player wins 80%+ of the time. No other factor — color, opening, or time control — comes close to this predictive power.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="finding-card">
      <h4>3. The Sicilian Fights Back</h4>
      <p>The Sicilian Defense is the only major opening where Black outperforms White (49.5% vs 45.6%). Black players seeking an active, fighting response to 1.e4 have data-backed justification for the Sicilian.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="finding-card">
      <h4>4. Avoid the Van't Kruijs</h4>
      <p>1.e3 produces a dismal 34.2% win rate for White — the worst of any opening with significant sample size. White players should avoid this passive opening in favor of principled first moves.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="finding-card">
      <h4>5. Clock Pressure is Color-Blind</h4>
      <p>Timeout finishes show no color advantage (49.7% White). Time management skill is independent of positional advantages, making clock discipline an equally important skill for both colors.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="finding-card">
      <h4>6. Stronger Players Play Longer</h4>
      <p>Average game length increases from 50 turns (&lt;1200) to 68 turns (2000+), reflecting deeper calculation, more resilient defense, and more patient conversion of advantages.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="finding-card">
      <h4>7. Moderate Opening Depth is Optimal</h4>
      <p>White's advantage peaks at 5–7 ply opening depth (51.9% WR). Shallow openings (1–2 ply) favor Black, while deep theory (12+ ply) equalizes. The sweet spot is recognizable, standard openings played to moderate depth.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="finding-card">
      <h4>8. Increment Changes the Game</h4>
      <p>Adding time increment dramatically reduces timeouts (15+0: 8.2% timeout vs 15+15: 3.6%) and increases draw rates. Coaches should consider increment-based time controls for development play.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="insight success" style="margin-top: 28px">
    <strong>For coaches and platform analysts:</strong> Rating differential is the single strongest predictor of game outcomes. Color advantage provides a modest but persistent edge that is most exploitable at intermediate levels. Opening selection matters most at the extremes — the Sicilian is Black's best weapon, while irregular openings like Van't Kruijs actively harm White. Time controls should be chosen deliberately: no-increment formats produce more decisive games but also more clock-related frustration.
  </div>
</section>

